    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "44383ba0316d59f3f88ee607e2717d5d",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"part-2-qt-k-succ-0-v-i_3\">Part 2: <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q^T K \\succ 0||)</marimo-tex>, <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(V = I_3||)</marimo-tex></h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">For <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q^T K \\succ 0||)</marimo-tex>, <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q^T K||)</marimo-tex> must be invertible, which implies that <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q, K||)</marimo-tex> are invertible as well. So we sample <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q||)</marimo-tex> uniformly on <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||([-1,1]||)</marimo-tex> with a bounded condition number <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\kappa(Q) &lt;= 10^4||)</marimo-tex> ensuring that <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q||)</marimo-tex> will be practically invertible. Then we sample a SPD matrix <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(M||)</marimo-tex> and define <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(K = Q^{-T} M||)</marimo-tex> so that <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(Q^T K = Q^T Q^{-T} M = M \\succ 0.||)</marimo-tex></span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
